  "whyItWorks": "A turnaround works like a run-up: everything in it leans toward beat 1 of the next chorus. The arpeggio climbs, the dominant adds its 3rd and b9, and the triplet speeds the line up just before the bar line. The two chromatic notes squeeze the target from both sides, so when the minor 3rd arrives it sounds like the only possible landing.",
  "fingeringNote": "Bar 1 is in eighth position in the lesson's original key, index finger across fret 8: roll it from string to string for the notes on that fret, and use the ring finger and pinky for the two higher frets. At bar 2 the index moves back one fret (marked as a shift), and the rest of the lick sits in seventh position, one finger per fret. In the triplet, pick the first note with the pinky, then pull off to the ring finger and to the index on the G string.",
  "practiceTask": "Play the lick over bars 15 and 16 of the weekly standard and let it land on bar 1 as the next chorus starts. Loop all three bars, then play the triplet alone, slowly, until the two pulled-off notes are as loud as the picked one.",
  "variation": {
    "description": "The variation keeps every note and changes the rhythm: the triplet moves to beat 3, so the 3rd, b9 and b7 of {{chord 3}} fly past as one quick arpeggio, and the enclosure ({{note 2.8}}, {{note 2.9}}) arrives on beat 4 in plain eighths, with a pull-off into {{note 2.9}}. Compare the two and keep the push into the next chorus you like better.",
